{! Writes to the files under {directory} fail as they do when the disk is full.

   "No space left on device" is what strerror(ENOSPC) says, which is the message of the IOException a write to a
   full disk fails with on Linux and macOS.

   $0.path is the path a stream or channel was opened with, as the application gave it, so it is made absolute
   before it is compared. A stream on a file descriptor, standard output for one, has none.

   Every rule records that it fired by setting the system property {hit}. !}

# every write of a FileOutputStream, and with it of a FileWriter or a PrintStream on a file
RULE {fault} FileOutputStream.write
CLASS java.io.FileOutputStream
METHOD write
AT ENTRY
IF $0.path != null && (new java.io.File($0.path)).toPath().toAbsolutePath().normalize().startsWith("{directory}")
DO java.lang.System.setProperty("{hit}", "true");
   throw new java.io.IOException("No space left on device")
ENDRULE
{#include hide-frames of="FileOutputStream.write" type="java.io.FileOutputStream" method="write" /}

RULE {fault} RandomAccessFile.write
CLASS java.io.RandomAccessFile
METHOD write
AT ENTRY
IF $0.path != null && (new java.io.File($0.path)).toPath().toAbsolutePath().normalize().startsWith("{directory}")
DO java.lang.System.setProperty("{hit}", "true");
   throw new java.io.IOException("No space left on device")
ENDRULE
{#include hide-frames of="RandomAccessFile.write" type="java.io.RandomAccessFile" method="write" /}

# the writes of a RandomAccessFile that do not go through write: writeBytes and writeChars
RULE {fault} RandomAccessFile.writeBytes
